  {
    "id": "10.1016/j.scriptamat.2026.117656",
    "venue": "j-bb97940e03",
    "year": 2026,
    "track": null,
    "title": "Modeling early-stage solid-state crystallization of amorphous LiCoO2 under flash lamp annealing",
    "authors": [
      "Yang Hu",
      "Wouter Vels",
      "Marta D. Rossell",
      "Vladyslav Turlo",
      "Yaroslav E. Romanyuk"
    ],
    "author_ids": [
      "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-0984-2871",
      "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8610-8853",
      "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8120-4643",
      "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0529-228X"
    ],
    "abstract": "The crystallinity of LiCoO2 (LCO) cathodes is essential for the performance of thin-film solid-state batteries. Flash-lamp annealing (FLA) can crystallize amorphous LCO within milliseconds, as a single 1.75 ms pulse heats the precursor to 1000–1500 K. Here we resolve the early stages of this rapid crystallization with isothermal molecular dynamics simulations at FLA-relevant temperatures using an Allegro machine-learning potential trained on stoichiometric LCO. The crystallization enthalpy is about -130 kJ/mol, negligible relative to the FLA pulse energy. Primary grains nucleate at random orientations, and most exhibit a nearly random distribution of Li and Co cations. With increasing temperature the trajectories pass from a nucleation-and-growth regime to Ostwald-style coarsening. Importantly, nucleation and initial crystallization occur within nanoseconds, far shorter than the FLA pulse duration.",
